  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nShower activity will slowly taper through the overnight hours with \nthe loss of daytime heating as our mid/upper-level trough continues \nto elongate across the state and advects drier air from the \nsouthwest. A surface high currently over the northern Gulf will \nprogress east as we go into Tuesday, which will allow a brief \nsoutherly to southeasterly return flow across our area. This, in \ncombination with enhanced mid/upper-level support associated with \nour trough and higher surface moisture levels, will allow for \ncontinued rain chances as we go into Tuesday. While not as quite as \nhigh as previous days, PWAT values will remain elevated closer to \nthe coastline (1.6-1.8\" range, which will support brief  periods of \nlocally heavier rainfall with any stronger storm, while inland \nlocations have lower PWAT values (1.1-1.5\") and thus inland \nlocations will have lower rainfall amounts. Additional rainfall \nthrough Tuesday evening of 0.5-1 inches are possible across the \nBrush Country/Rio Grande Plains with 1-2 inches closer to the \nCoastal Bend. Lower rain chances (20-30%) will exist early Wednesday \nmorning along the coast as the main forcing associated with the \ntrough moves east and dry air filters in. \n\nOur next cold front looks to arrive sometime Thursday bringing \ncooler temperatures and lower humidity, but model discrepancies on \nexact timing and intensity do remain at this time. We will have a \nbetter grasp on exact details oncer there is better agreement on \nmodel consensus. Nonetheless, cooler and drier conditions will \nprevail through Friday. Another mid-level trough looks to dig across \nthe Southwestern US/northwestern Mexico this weekend bringing the \nnext chance for rain.\n\nThe overall risk for minor coastal flooding will continue through \nthis morning. P-ETSS guidance forecasts water levels reaching near \n1.7 ft above MSL through the upcoming tide cycles but with the lack \nof higher wave periods, not sure there'll be enough of a push to \ncreate enough runup to cause additional concerns.",
